Question 3 [10]
 Create Sequence Diagram
This on-demand custom cake and bakery service allows consumers to order personalized cakes, cupcakes, and other baked goods. 
Consumers can choose from a variety of cake types such as chocolate, vanilla, or red velvet and customize them with different frosting flavors and decorations. 
If a consumer has a specific design in mind, they can upload an image file for custom decoration. 
To place an order, consumers must create an account and provide their details, including their shipping address and payment method.
After selecting the cake type and any customizations, the system generates a preview of the cake and calculates the total cost. 
If any required information is missing, such as payment or address details, the consumer is prompted to provide the necessary information. 
Upon confirming the order, payment is processed, and once it is approved, the bakery team proceeds with the preparation. 
If the uploaded design or other aspects of the order do not meet the bakery’s standards, the consumer is informed and asked to submit a new design. 
After the cake is baked and decorated, it is delivered to the specified address. 
The system also tracks ingredients like flour and frosting, and when supplies are running low, it automatically orders more from the suppliers.



Answer:
Here’s a UML Sequence Diagram (in PlantUML syntax) covering the end-to-end flow you described. Copy the block into any PlantUML renderer to visualize the sequence:

@startuml
actor Consumer
participant "CustomCakeSystem" as System
participant PaymentGateway
participant BakeryTeam
participant InventorySystem
participant Supplier
participant DeliveryService

== Account Creation & Details ==
Consumer -> System: createAccount()
alt missing shipping/payment info
    System --> Consumer: promptForMissingInfo()
    Consumer -> System: submitShippingAndPaymentInfo()
end

== Customization & Preview ==
Consumer -> System: selectCake(type, frosting, decorations)
alt custom design required
    Consumer -> System: uploadDesign(imageFile)
end
System -> System: generatePreview()
System -> System: calculateTotalCost()
System --> Consumer: showPreviewAndCost()

== Order Confirmation & Payment ==
Consumer -> System: confirmOrder()
System -> PaymentGateway: processPayment(paymentDetails)
alt payment approved
    PaymentGateway --> System: paymentSuccess()
    
    == Production Phase ==
    System -> BakeryTeam: newOrder(orderDetails)
    BakeryTeam -> System: validateDesign()
    alt design issue
        System --> Consumer: notifyDesignIssue()
        Consumer -> System: resubmitDesign(imageFile)
        BakeryTeam -> System: designApproved()
    end
    
    == Ingredient Check & Reorder ==
    BakeryTeam -> InventorySystem: checkIngredientStock()
    alt stock < threshold
        InventorySystem -> Supplier: placeReorder(materials)
        Supplier --> InventorySystem: confirmReorder()
    end
    
    == Bake & Decorate ==
    BakeryTeam -> BakeryTeam: bakeCake()
    BakeryTeam -> BakeryTeam: decorateCake()
    
    == Delivery ==
    BakeryTeam -> DeliveryService: scheduleDelivery(order)
    DeliveryService --> Consumer: deliverCake()
    DeliveryService --> System: deliveryConfirmed()

else payment failed
    PaymentGateway --> System: paymentFailure()
    System --> Consumer: notifyPaymentFailure()
end
@enduml


Explanation of key segments:

Account setup

Consumer creates an account.

If shipping or payment details are missing, the system prompts and the consumer supplies them.

Customization & preview

Consumer selects cake parameters (type, frosting, decorations).

Optionally uploads a custom design image.

System generates a preview and computes the cost, then displays both.

Payment & order confirmation

Consumer confirms the order, triggering payment processing via an external gateway.

On success, flow continues to production; on failure, consumer is notified.

Production

Bakery team validates the design. If there’s an issue, the consumer is notified and can resubmit.

Ingredients are checked; low-stock triggers an automatic reorder from suppliers.

Baking, decorating & delivery

Cake is baked and decorated.

Delivery is scheduled; the delivery service delivers the cake and confirms back to the system.

This sequence captures all the interactions, decision points (alt/else), and lifelines (actors/components) in your custom cake ordering process.
